From d427e1211152d2f265f092bd35281fb521c1c00a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: primez <mpincoski@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 13:42:45 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] [Session] Apply a seek at a pending quality change after the
 reopen

- A seek while a stream waits for the reopen of a quality change keeps the
  seek time. The old reader has no data for TimeSeek, so the seek ran on the
  old reader, failed, and the new reader started at the segment start. The
  session now ends the old reader, moves the stream to the segment of the
  seek, and seeks again once the new readers are open (OnDemuxRead, as for a
  seek across a period).
- Segment, period and PTS offset times convert between timescales with
  PLAYLIST::ConvertTime (quotient and remainder, no overflow for 32-bit
  timescales, rounded down as the session time line). The PTS offsets,
  getMaxTimeMs, GetChapterPos, the subtitle reader and the manifest parsers
  multiplied before the division: at a high timescale the product overflowed,
  and a seek chose the wrong segment. The unused SetScaling code is removed.
- A DASH SegmentList without a timescale uses 1 (seconds), as a
  SegmentTemplate does. The timescale 0 counted the segment times as
  microseconds, and the exact seek of 8509ade divided by zero.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
